----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, N: b8-d7, B: c8-d7, B: c8-e6, B: c8-f5, B: c8-g4, B: c8-h3, P: d5-d4, Q: d8-c7, Q: d8-b6, Q: d8-a5, Q: d8-d7, Q: d8-d6, P: e7-e5, P: e7-e6, K: e8-d7, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation: Several promising moves are available. The pawn push P: e7-e5 opens lines for the bishop on c8 and queen, potentially increasing activity. However, it may also block the bishop if not timed well. The knight move N: b8-c6 develops a piece with central influence and supports potential pressure on d4 or e5. The bishop move B: c8-g4 pins the knight on f3 if possible (but here it would be eyeing g4 with pressure on the center). The queen move Q: d8-b6 puts immediate pressure on the weakened white queenside, especially targeting the b2-pawn and aligning with the a1 rook via diagonal. Notably, White has weakened dark squares around their king (especially f2, g2) due to lack of kingside development.\n\nAnother key observation: White's king is still in the center (KWF1), and Black has relatively faster development with more minor pieces ready to act. Pushing P: d5-d4 challenges the center immediately and gains space, but after d5-d4, White can play e2-e4 or exd4, possibly opening the position while Black’s king is still in the center.\n\nAlternatively, developing the knight with N: b8-c6 is solid, natural, increases control over central squares, prepares for possible ...e6 or ...e5 breaks, and doesn’t weaken any squares.\n\nHowever, Q: d8-b6 stands out because it applies immediate pressure on b2 (undefended), threatens to win material if not addressed, and discourages White from castling queenside comfortably. It also connects with potential battery ideas along the a5-e1 diagonal later.\n\nReason: Q: d8-b6 exerts immediate pressure on White’s position, targets the vulnerable b2 pawn, limits White’s development options, and forces a response, giving Black the initiative. It’s more aggressive than slow development moves and capitalizes on White’s underdeveloped queenside.\n\nAnswer: Q: d8-b6"}
